I think it doesn't have
to be a morning routine,

00:22:50.595 --> 00:22:51.982
for a lot of people it is because

00:22:51.982 --> 00:22:53.938
get it done in the morning you
don't have to worry about it,

00:22:53.938 --> 00:22:56.406
but some people are more
active in the evenings,

00:22:56.406 --> 00:22:58.586
so know when you're time
is, that's the first thing.

00:22:58.586 --> 00:23:00.737
Know when you are most focused,

00:23:00.737 --> 00:23:03.271
most creative, and most alive.

00:23:03.271 --> 00:23:05.274
Some people are night
owls, do it at night;

00:23:05.274 --> 00:23:08.417
if you're a morning person,
do it in the morning.

00:23:08.417 --> 00:23:12.051
For us, we love to mix
breath, because again,

00:23:12.051 --> 00:23:13.895
most people don't know how to breathe.

00:23:13.895 --> 00:23:15.565
This sounds so simple,
most people are like,

00:23:15.565 --> 00:23:17.092
"Yeah okay, breath, whatever."

00:23:17.092 --> 00:23:19.432
The minute you tap into
the power of your breath,

00:23:19.432 --> 00:23:22.497
you are literally tapping
into the life force energy.

00:23:22.497 --> 00:23:24.166
That is life.

00:23:24.166 --> 00:23:25.413
- Why don't we do it now?

00:23:25.413 --> 00:23:27.137
Teach me how to breathe.
- What's that?

00:23:27.137 --> 00:23:27.970
- Teach us how to breathe.

00:23:27.970 --> 00:23:29.003
- All right, you want to know?

00:23:29.003 --> 00:23:31.305
All right, we're going to
show you how to center,

00:23:31.305 --> 00:23:32.742
you're standing up, yeah?

00:23:32.742 --> 00:23:33.979
- [Evan] Yeah.

00:23:33.979 --> 00:23:35.049
- I'm going to stand up.

00:23:35.049 --> 00:23:38.996
So center, put your finger,
your thumb in your belly button.

00:23:38.996 --> 00:23:41.400
- Okay, which one, does it matter?

00:23:41.400 --> 00:23:42.233
- Okay, left hand.
- Yeah, just your thumb

00:23:42.233 --> 00:23:43.406
in your belly button.

00:23:43.406 --> 00:23:46.457
And then wherever your pinky finger falls,

00:23:46.457 --> 00:23:48.019
that is the center-point of your body.

00:23:48.019 --> 00:23:51.626
So where your hand is, you want
to breathe into that space,

00:23:51.626 --> 00:23:54.021
and that is called your center,
that is your center-point.

00:23:54.021 --> 00:23:56.049
So, breathe into it so
that your stomach expands.

00:23:56.049 --> 00:23:58.184
- You want to try to look pregnant.

00:23:58.184 --> 00:24:00.184
- All right, okay, good.

00:24:02.006 --> 00:24:04.366
- [Alexi] So, see how it's moving?

00:24:04.366 --> 00:24:05.533
- Yeah.
- Good.

00:24:06.851 --> 00:24:08.921
So now, we're going to
teach you how to stand

00:24:08.921 --> 00:24:11.857
in a grounded position, so
feet shoulder-width apart.

00:24:11.857 --> 00:24:13.909
- Shoulders up and back.

00:24:13.909 --> 00:24:16.188
- Yep, shoulders up and back, so like--

00:24:16.188 --> 00:24:18.785
- Jaw released.
- Jaw released here,

00:24:18.785 --> 00:24:20.300
so that the air is completing through,

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:22.911
'cause most people with
stress, they lockjaw,

00:24:22.911 --> 00:24:24.856
and they hold all the energy.

00:24:24.856 --> 00:24:26.077
- Okay.
- So, with your mouth

00:24:26.077 --> 00:24:28.570
slightly open, just keep
breathing into that space,

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:32.767
knees slightly bent. (breathes deeply)

00:24:32.767 --> 00:24:35.020
Breathing into that space.

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:36.484
Now feel how like,

00:24:36.484 --> 00:24:38.366
feel how much choice you have in movement,

00:24:38.366 --> 00:24:40.572
you're grounded, but you can move, right?

00:24:40.572 --> 00:24:41.975
- Yep, yeah.
- If my feet were

00:24:41.975 --> 00:24:46.025
close together, I can't do
that, I'm going to fall over.

00:24:46.025 --> 00:24:48.034
So, this is the a physical metaphor

00:24:48.034 --> 00:24:49.373
for what's happening in your life:

00:24:49.373 --> 00:24:51.542
when your feet are in a grounded position,

00:24:51.542 --> 00:24:55.181
you're in your physical center,
you've relaxed your jaw,

00:24:55.181 --> 00:24:57.347
you're actually creating the space

00:24:57.347 --> 00:25:01.354
for full range of movement without moving.

00:25:01.354 --> 00:25:04.939
That's when you become like
Neo in the Matrix, right?

00:25:04.939 --> 00:25:06.855
When you're grounded,
(Evan laughing)

00:25:06.855 --> 00:25:11.070
and fully-centered, you have
space and you have choice,

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:13.903
so we do centering, that exercise,

00:25:14.787 --> 00:25:15.960
When we first started doing it,

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:17.866
and first started learning breath work,

00:25:17.866 --> 00:25:22.196
we put little centering reminders
in our phone every hour.

00:25:22.196 --> 00:25:24.692
So, we'd be like at our
computer workin' on something,

00:25:24.692 --> 00:25:27.327
building out our business,
centering alarm would go off,

00:25:27.327 --> 00:25:31.000
we would just stop in our
chair, reset our bodies,

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:34.596
(exhales) drop our breath,
drop our jaw, recenter.

00:25:34.596 --> 00:25:35.844
Now it's a practice for us,

00:25:35.844 --> 00:25:39.293
we probably center 3,000 times a day.

00:25:39.293 --> 00:25:40.946
And it's so interesting,

00:25:40.946 --> 00:25:43.384
'cause we teach this in our workshops,

00:25:43.384 --> 00:25:46.202
when people come in day one
they don't know how to center,

00:25:46.202 --> 00:25:47.738
they come up in front of the room,

00:25:47.738 --> 00:25:49.470
and they're completely like nervous,

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:50.434
and scratching their head,

00:25:50.434 --> 00:25:52.613
and doing weird things with their bodies.

00:25:52.613 --> 00:25:53.909
Day two they walk out and they're like,

00:25:53.909 --> 00:25:56.316
"Hi, here's who I am, here's
what I'm committed to,

00:25:56.316 --> 00:25:59.513
"here's my vision," and
it's all because of breath.

00:25:59.513 --> 00:26:02.167
Literally, it's the simplest
thing, but it's everything.

00:26:02.167 --> 00:26:04.224
- Yeah, but there's also more to that,

00:26:04.224 --> 00:26:06.325
because we go into some really deep stuff,

00:26:06.325 --> 00:26:07.283
and that's one of the things

00:26:07.283 --> 00:26:10.183
that everybody gets to understand,

00:26:10.183 --> 00:26:12.306
is the body is a living library.

00:26:12.306 --> 00:26:14.111
It's storing all of your traumas,

00:26:14.111 --> 00:26:16.388
everything you've ever been through

00:26:16.388 --> 00:26:17.694
is happening in the body.

00:26:17.694 --> 00:26:22.355
And so, what tends to happen
is, is people are unaware

00:26:22.355 --> 00:26:25.744
because they can't see themselves,

00:26:25.744 --> 00:26:28.371
they can't see themselves when
they are in fight, flight,

00:26:28.371 --> 00:26:32.204
freeze, or appease, as
we have added in there.

00:26:33.133 --> 00:26:35.133
And so, the breath, yes,

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:40.155
and cleaning up your own consciousness

00:26:40.155 --> 00:26:44.164
by facing off with the parts of yourself

00:26:44.164 --> 00:26:46.496
that you've been hiding from.

00:26:46.496 --> 00:26:48.726
That's the work, especially that work,

00:26:48.726 --> 00:26:50.777
that most people aren't willing to do,

00:26:50.777 --> 00:26:52.329
but those who do it are the ones

00:26:52.329 --> 00:26:54.679
who experience the most freedom.

00:26:54.679 --> 00:26:58.349
- Yeah, 'cause they clear
space for more to come in.

00:26:58.349 --> 00:26:59.809
I think a lot of people don't realize

00:26:59.809 --> 00:27:01.942
how much crap they're holding onto,

00:27:01.942 --> 00:27:04.198
even simple, small things from childhood,

00:27:04.198 --> 00:27:06.675
like you were told to shut
up when you were singing

00:27:06.675 --> 00:27:07.859
when you were eight years old,

00:27:07.859 --> 00:27:10.159
so now you don't fully use your voice.

00:27:10.159 --> 00:27:11.878
There's so many small, little traumas

00:27:11.878 --> 00:27:13.656
that happen throughout our lives,

00:27:13.656 --> 00:27:17.259
that are actually in the
way of us fully leading.

00:27:17.259 --> 00:27:19.394
- I like it, I like it.

00:27:19.394 --> 00:27:21.049
More questions, comin' up:

00:27:21.049 --> 00:27:24.099
can you expand on the
topic of consistency,

00:27:24.099 --> 00:27:27.009
how do I stay consistent in my actions?

00:27:27.009 --> 00:27:28.635
What do you think?

00:27:28.635 --> 00:27:30.968
- Simple answer, just do it.

00:27:31.846 --> 00:27:36.013
Consistency it is literally
the game-changer for everyone.

00:27:37.419 --> 00:27:38.473
People come to us and say,

00:27:38.473 --> 00:27:40.777
"I want to build a tribe like
you've got, you guys have built,

00:27:40.777 --> 00:27:42.723
"I want to build a business
like you guys have built,"

00:27:42.723 --> 00:27:45.613
we have been consistently in the gym

00:27:45.613 --> 00:27:47.173
on our own consciousness,

00:27:47.173 --> 00:27:50.226
and in the gym building our
business every single day

00:27:50.226 --> 00:27:53.967
for the last decade, and people
want it overnight, (snaps),

00:27:53.967 --> 00:27:57.941
and it's the consistent
action that produces a result.

00:27:57.941 --> 00:27:59.579
So, how do you be more consistent?

00:27:59.579 --> 00:28:02.304
You schedule it in, you
make it non-negotiable,

00:28:02.304 --> 00:28:05.094
you get over your reasons,
and you get unreasonable,

00:28:05.094 --> 00:28:06.819
and make it happen no matter what.

00:28:06.819 --> 00:28:08.359
Even when you don't feel like it,

00:28:08.359 --> 00:28:11.135
like we're up at 7:30 in
the morning for this call.

00:28:11.135 --> 00:28:13.279
I was up at seven yesterday,

00:28:13.279 --> 00:28:15.103
we do an online master mind program

00:28:15.103 --> 00:28:16.574
with our people here in Australia;

00:28:16.574 --> 00:28:18.086
once we get back in LA,

00:28:18.086 --> 00:28:20.975
we're up at 3:00 a.m. in the
morning to do their calls.

00:28:20.975 --> 00:28:22.610
Get unreasonable!

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:24.016
Do whatever it takes to make it happen,

00:28:24.016 --> 00:28:26.502
and most people are unwilling to do that.

00:28:26.502 --> 00:28:30.669
- Yeah, and one of the things
that I'll add to that is,

00:28:32.073 --> 00:28:33.990
celebrate when you are.

00:28:35.177 --> 00:28:38.584
Because what you appreciate, appreciates;

00:28:38.584 --> 00:28:41.080
what you celebrate, gets repeated.

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:43.600
And so one of the things, and I get it,

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:45.831
'cause I would be the person
hearing this and going,

00:28:45.831 --> 00:28:48.507
"Okay consistency, but like
how the hell do I do it?"

00:28:48.507 --> 00:28:51.153
Well, whatever you practice
more of, you get more of.

00:28:51.153 --> 00:28:55.763
So, if you are practicing
micro-wins, small wins,

00:28:55.763 --> 00:28:56.802
every time you do a small win,

00:28:56.802 --> 00:29:01.595
every time you do and be what
you say you would do and be,

00:29:01.595 --> 00:29:04.968
every single time, celebrate that.

00:29:04.968 --> 00:29:06.166
Take a moment, stop, and just go:

00:29:06.166 --> 00:29:10.061
wow, today I sent every
email that I said I would,

00:29:10.061 --> 00:29:12.067
or at least half of them, wow;

00:29:12.067 --> 00:29:13.722
today I reached up to my mom

00:29:13.722 --> 00:29:15.784
and I told her I loved her, wow;

00:29:15.784 --> 00:29:19.162
today I did my bookkeeping,
which I've been hating.

00:29:19.162 --> 00:29:20.849
- (chuckles) Avoiding. (laughs)

00:29:20.849 --> 00:29:21.832
- Wow!

00:29:21.832 --> 00:29:24.461
- You're like me, (laughs), and him.

00:29:24.461 --> 00:29:26.607
- So now, I get to celebrate that.

00:29:26.607 --> 00:29:28.644
And I don't mean celebrate it
by going and watching porn,

00:29:28.644 --> 00:29:30.840
or like drinking, and all of that stuff,

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:35.007
because those also are
habits, and here's the thing:

00:29:35.866 --> 00:29:37.825
there are certain habits,
and we've been talking

00:29:37.825 --> 00:29:41.987
about the five success habits,
and there's two more to go,

00:29:41.987 --> 00:29:43.407
but there are certain habits

00:29:43.407 --> 00:29:47.240
that are counter-productive
to success habits.

00:29:48.672 --> 00:29:50.922
One being alcohol and vices

00:29:52.898 --> 00:29:55.539
that we use to numb ourselves out,

00:29:55.539 --> 00:29:58.956
a lot of people are trying to avoid life,

00:30:00.471 --> 00:30:04.217
and life is just life, it's impartial.

00:30:04.217 --> 00:30:06.980
It's not promised that it's
always going to be good,

00:30:06.980 --> 00:30:09.063
because good and bad
are mental constructs,

00:30:09.063 --> 00:30:12.301
they are not True, with a capital T.

00:30:12.301 --> 00:30:13.209
Right and wrong, good and bad,

00:30:13.209 --> 00:30:15.876
these are things right in Australia,

00:30:15.876 --> 00:30:17.908
could be wrong in Pakistan,

00:30:17.908 --> 00:30:21.327
all of that is up to
the eye of the beholder.

00:30:21.327 --> 00:30:23.371
The process that a lot of
people find themselves in

00:30:23.371 --> 00:30:26.997
is they're hiding from themselves.

00:30:26.997 --> 00:30:28.220
- And the minute stuff comes up,

00:30:28.220 --> 00:30:30.003
and the minute life gets tough,

00:30:30.003 --> 00:30:31.777
a lot of people go to
their numbing agents,

00:30:31.777 --> 00:30:34.225
whether it be TV, or scrolling
through social media,

00:30:34.225 --> 00:30:39.164
or porn, or food, or alcohol,
whatever it might be.

00:30:39.164 --> 00:30:40.247
- Swiping through Tinder

00:30:40.247 --> 00:30:41.080
and Bumble.
- People are numbing

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:43.689
themselves out with these distractions,

00:30:43.689 --> 00:30:45.761
and then we wonder why we're not achieving

00:30:45.761 --> 00:30:46.655
the life that we want,

00:30:46.655 --> 00:30:48.461
it's because we're completely distracted

00:30:48.461 --> 00:30:50.839
and shut off from who we really are.

00:30:50.839 --> 00:30:52.942
And that brings us into the fourth step,

00:30:52.942 --> 00:30:56.422
which is truly owning who you
are, and that means all of it,

00:30:56.422 --> 00:30:58.357
not just like, oh I'm going to own

00:30:58.357 --> 00:31:01.496
that I'm "all your good traits",
and list your good traits,

00:31:01.496 --> 00:31:03.249
I'm this, I'm that, I'm that.

00:31:03.249 --> 00:31:06.222
It's I'm going to own who
I am, and that includes

00:31:06.222 --> 00:31:09.717
all the things that I've
been resisting for years;

00:31:09.717 --> 00:31:12.034
that includes all the things
that I've been afraid of

00:31:12.034 --> 00:31:12.959
and I've been running from.

00:31:12.959 --> 00:31:16.555
It's owning your totality
and accepting your totality,

00:31:16.555 --> 00:31:18.563
and then living from that totality.

00:31:18.563 --> 00:31:20.925
'Cause most people are
trying to be somebody,

00:31:20.925 --> 00:31:22.667
they're trying to be somebody else,

00:31:22.667 --> 00:31:24.801
they're trying to be Evan,
they're trying to be Preston,

00:31:24.801 --> 00:31:25.715
they're trying to be Alexi,

00:31:25.715 --> 00:31:27.476
and trying to be the person, Gary V.,

00:31:27.476 --> 00:31:31.202
they're trying to be somebody
that they see online so,

00:31:31.202 --> 00:31:32.837
"I got to do it like they do it,"

00:31:32.837 --> 00:31:34.014
and they're missing the point.

00:31:34.014 --> 00:31:37.268
The minute you become yourself,
you actually become unique,

00:31:37.268 --> 00:31:40.809
you actually become worth
getting to know because

00:31:40.809 --> 00:31:43.871
you're different from the
rest of the sheeple out there,

00:31:43.871 --> 00:31:46.567
and most people, they miss this.

00:31:46.567 --> 00:31:48.701
Well, in my attempt to
try and be somebody,

00:31:48.701 --> 00:31:51.403
you've missed the point, and
you've lost your uniqueness,

00:31:51.403 --> 00:31:53.261
and when you own your totality,

00:31:53.261 --> 00:31:57.084
you become something really interesting.

00:31:57.084 --> 00:31:58.499
And that's what people don't get,

00:31:58.499 --> 00:32:02.383
especially in this Internet
culture of comparison,

00:32:02.383 --> 00:32:04.553
and looking at so-and-so online,

00:32:04.553 --> 00:32:06.837
and the minute you drop
into the self, you win.

00:32:06.837 --> 00:32:07.670
You win.

00:32:09.026 --> 00:32:11.938
- I like the sheeple
comment, that was good.

00:32:11.938 --> 00:32:15.337
I'm curious for you guys,
so like you're both,

00:32:15.337 --> 00:32:17.115
you're a couple, you're in the business,

00:32:17.115 --> 00:32:18.285
you're both delivering the training,

00:32:18.285 --> 00:32:21.031
you're both on this call, how do you guys

00:32:21.031 --> 00:32:24.394
split up the content, and
like who's better at what?

00:32:24.394 --> 00:32:26.346
And how do you share the delivery?

00:32:26.346 --> 00:32:28.893
'Cause this is great, we ask a question,

00:32:28.893 --> 00:32:30.673
you're both givin' an
answer, but I'm curious,

00:32:30.673 --> 00:32:32.533
what are the strengths that
you feel like you both bring,

00:32:32.533 --> 00:32:35.459
and maybe the weaknesses,
too, to your seminars,

00:32:35.459 --> 00:32:39.626
to your events, to the
content that you're creating?

00:32:41.393 --> 00:32:42.726
- You know, that's a tricky thing,

00:32:42.726 --> 00:32:45.582
'cause it's always
moving, always changing.

00:32:45.582 --> 00:32:49.710
And full transparency, that
definitely comes up for us,

00:32:49.710 --> 00:32:52.171
because we're both loaded weapons.

00:32:52.171 --> 00:32:55.671
We're both A-type personalities, you know?

00:32:56.666 --> 00:32:58.451
Even in this call I watch my ego flare up,

00:32:58.451 --> 00:33:02.311
and want to like, "Oh, she
answered twice, and I didn't!"

00:33:02.311 --> 00:33:04.253
And like, so what?
- Yeah, yeah, it happens.

00:33:04.253 --> 00:33:06.246
- So what, ya know?

00:33:06.246 --> 00:33:08.960
For us it's what are we here for?

00:33:08.960 --> 00:33:12.995
And the answer to that all
day, every day, is service.

00:33:12.995 --> 00:33:14.789
I could give two (cuts out)

00:33:14.789 --> 00:33:17.329
whether it's her answering
all the questions,

00:33:17.329 --> 00:33:19.845
or me answering them or whatever,

00:33:19.845 --> 00:33:23.529
it's less about that and more
about what are we here for?

00:33:23.529 --> 00:33:26.946
And so, sometimes one of us gets on fire,

00:33:28.301 --> 00:33:33.234
and at that point, sometimes
the other ego flares a second,

00:33:33.234 --> 00:33:35.234
and then we recognize it,
and then go right back

00:33:35.234 --> 00:33:37.593
to what is here, what
is the point of this?

00:33:37.593 --> 00:33:39.344
The point of this is to serve people,

00:33:39.344 --> 00:33:44.121
and so if Alexi is on fire,
then she gets to stay on fire,

00:33:44.121 --> 00:33:46.420
and I get to hold space
because here's the thing

00:33:46.420 --> 00:33:48.229
that's tricky about our society:

00:33:48.229 --> 00:33:50.123
everybody wants to be the LeBron James,

00:33:50.123 --> 00:33:52.815
everybody wants to be
Oprah Winfrey, right?

00:33:52.815 --> 00:33:56.982
But there's a way to do
that by just holding space,

00:33:58.348 --> 00:33:59.655
just being your loving self.

00:33:59.655 --> 00:34:02.722
It's not about words, ya know?

00:34:02.722 --> 00:34:07.632
We get it so confused, we
think it's do, have, be,

00:34:07.632 --> 00:34:09.559
but the truth is it's be, do, have.

00:34:09.559 --> 00:34:13.493
And so, in the being-ness,
I can still be a space

00:34:13.493 --> 00:34:14.833
for transformation, personal development,

00:34:14.833 --> 00:34:17.676
and for people to see that
when you amplify your love,

00:34:17.676 --> 00:34:19.749
you automatically amplify your life

00:34:19.749 --> 00:34:21.897
without saying a damn word.

00:34:21.897 --> 00:34:23.865
- Yeah, and that's real leadership.

00:34:23.865 --> 00:34:27.448
We have a leadership
"test" in our program,

00:34:28.470 --> 00:34:30.704
where people don't even
know what's happening,

00:34:30.704 --> 00:34:32.888
and it's awesome.

00:34:32.888 --> 00:34:36.326
And what happens is, for us we're kind of

00:34:36.326 --> 00:34:38.834
sitting on the sidelines and
waiting to see what happens,

00:34:38.834 --> 00:34:40.956
'cause chaos always ensues,

00:34:40.956 --> 00:34:43.710
and then leadership starts to emerge.

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:46.146
And we start to watch the
different types of leadership,

00:34:46.146 --> 00:34:48.607
there's always the
bull-headed leader who's like,

00:34:48.607 --> 00:34:50.609
"I've got the loudest voice, listen to me,

00:34:50.609 --> 00:34:53.203
"I'm going to control this situation."

00:34:53.203 --> 00:34:55.303
And then there's the
leader who's the quiet one,

00:34:55.303 --> 00:34:58.391
who's actually empowering others to lead,

00:34:58.391 --> 00:34:59.767
and to do what needs to be done

00:34:59.767 --> 00:35:01.904
to get the job done as a team.

00:35:01.904 --> 00:35:05.084
And that, in our definition,
is what true leadership is,

00:35:05.084 --> 00:35:08.426
is allowing others to rise
into their leadership.

